1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:07,130
Every timber in a frame is marked and
cut on its own, before the frame goes up.

2
00:00:07,620 --> 00:00:10,970
So how does each piece
know where it belongs?

3
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:18,090
The answer starts on a board, and ends in
a short written address on every member.

4
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:28,330
This is an itazu, a board plan.

5
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:32,920
It's the carpenter's own drawing
of the building, made on a thin

6
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:38,150
board, today often plywood, and
drawn from the architect's plans.

7
00:00:38,820 --> 00:00:40,510
It goes by other names too:

8
00:00:41,160 --> 00:00:43,250
zuita, or teita.

9
00:00:43,633 --> 00:00:45,863
It is not a detailed drawing.

10
00:00:46,333 --> 00:00:50,563
It works like a floor plan and
the framing plans combined:

11
00:00:51,113 --> 00:00:55,863
where every post stands, and which
beams and sills run between them.

12
00:00:56,133 --> 00:00:58,483
The board is ruled into a grid.

13
00:00:59,073 --> 00:01:03,888
The lines are usually three
shaku apart, about nine hundred

14
00:01:03,928 --> 00:01:08,423
ten millimeters, and the
posts stand at the crossings.

15
00:01:09,373 --> 00:01:13,303
Each grid line runs through
the centers of the posts on it.

16
00:01:17,433 --> 00:01:20,303
Then every grid line gets a name.

17
00:01:20,673 --> 00:01:23,083
This system is called banzuke.

18
00:01:23,653 --> 00:01:27,193
In its classic form, one
direction is labeled with

19
00:01:27,233 --> 00:01:33,123
the old Japanese syllable
order, i, ro, ha, ni.

20
00:01:33,753 --> 00:01:37,363
The other is numbered, one, two, three.

21
00:01:37,667 --> 00:01:40,657
Now every crossing has an address.

22
00:01:41,207 --> 00:01:46,257
The corner post here is i-no-ichi,
written with two characters.

23
00:01:46,887 --> 00:01:49,346
The next one along is ro-no-ichi.

24
00:01:53,633 --> 00:01:57,173
One common layout runs
the syllables across the

25
00:01:57,213 --> 00:02:01,083
board and counts the
numbers down from the top.

26
00:02:01,453 --> 00:02:04,953
But which way the axes
go, and which corner

27
00:02:04,993 --> 00:02:08,543
starts, varies by region and by carpenter.

28
00:02:09,253 --> 00:02:11,463
Other numbering systems exist too.

29
00:02:12,167 --> 00:02:15,157
Now the plan comes off the board.

30
00:02:15,727 --> 00:02:19,587
Marking out each member, the
carpenter checks the board

31
00:02:19,627 --> 00:02:23,237
and writes that member's
address on it, by hand.

32
00:02:24,027 --> 00:02:27,907
The address says where the
piece goes; the story stick

33
00:02:27,947 --> 00:02:32,337
gives its heights; the center
line gives the reference.

34
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:37,850
The address goes where it will be
hidden once the frame is built.

35
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:45,870
On a tenon, say, or on the part of
a post that ends up below the floor.

36
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:53,140
In one common practice,
every piece that joins a

37
00:02:53,180 --> 00:02:56,390
post carries the same
address at that joint.

38
00:02:56,960 --> 00:03:01,370
The beam that meets post i-no-ichi
is marked i-no-ichi right there.

39
00:03:01,667 --> 00:03:07,167
And every member gets its center
line, struck in ink, so the layout

40
00:03:07,207 --> 00:03:11,197
is measured from the center,
just as in the first episode.

41
00:03:11,900 --> 00:03:15,240
The same addresses serve again when an old

42
00:03:15,280 --> 00:03:19,350
building is taken apart
to be repaired or moved:

43
00:03:19,820 --> 00:03:23,510
every piece can go back
exactly where it came from.

44
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:28,790
So the work runs in one direction.

45
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:30,510
Plan on the board.

46
00:03:31,100 --> 00:03:32,850
Story sticks for the heights.

47
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:35,270
Marking out each member.

48
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:38,750
Then the cutting, called kizami.

49
00:03:39,100 --> 00:03:42,440
When the frame is raised,
the crew reads the

50
00:03:42,480 --> 00:03:45,830
addresses, and matching
labels go together.

51
00:03:50,218 --> 00:03:53,283
Hand-drawn board plans are rare today.

52
00:03:53,853 --> 00:03:57,153
By twenty fifteen, about nine in ten

53
00:03:57,193 --> 00:04:00,823
post-and-beam frames in
Japan were cut by machine.

54
00:04:01,473 --> 00:04:06,723
But the logic is the same, and it's what
you need for cutting joints by hand.

55
00:04:09,033 --> 00:04:11,183
Next time, the cutting kit:

56
00:04:11,653 --> 00:04:18,423
the saws, the chisels and the hammer, the
plane, and what it means to cut to a line.

57
00:04:19,433 --> 00:04:21,563
Draw it once on the board.

58
00:04:22,133 --> 00:04:24,303
Give every crossing an address.

59
00:04:24,913 --> 00:04:27,183
Write the address on every piece.

60
00:04:27,893 --> 00:04:30,903
Then the frame tells you
how it goes together.
